<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Culhuacan</h1><p class="st-meta">1150 CE – 1521 CE (approx.) · capital Culhuacan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An ancient altepetl on the southern shore of Lake Texcoco, prized for its Toltec royal lineage, which passed legitimacy to the Mexica kings.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Culhuacan was settled in the Classic period and became a major city after the fall of Tula around 1150, when Toltec nobles were said to have established a dynasty there. It dominated the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a> in the 12th and 13th centuries.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its rulers claimed direct Toltec descent, making Culhua lineage highly prestigious. In the early 14th century, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> tradition, the ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coxcox">Coxcox</a> allowed the wandering Mexica to settle at Tizaapan as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>; they fought for him but were expelled after sacrificing a Culhua princess. The first Mexica king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acamapichtli">Acamapichtli</a> had Culhua royal ancestry.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Culhuacan was weakened by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tepanec-empire">Tepanec Empire</a> and absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> in the 15th century. It was conquered by the Spanish in 1521 and is now a district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 1150 CE – 1521 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:82.59%;width:7.38%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1150 CE – 1521 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Culhuacan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Coxcox</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mesoamerican religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Nahuatl</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Colhuacan, Culhuacán</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1336&amp;focus=culhuacan" aria-label="Culhuacan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1962 1304 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Culhuacan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2417 1744h0M2320 1712h0M2322 1714h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2282 1560h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M2282 1560h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Mexico. <a href="/atlas?year=1336&amp;focus=culhuacan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
